  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>On the road linking Gassin to Saint-Tropez, Le Treizain beach offers an open view of the gulf, with Saint-Tropez and Sainte-Maxime in the background. It is one of the three beaches of the town of Gassin, with free access, described as a "nature beach" by the tourist office.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The Gassin tourist office page (updated on 3 September 2025) says "animals accepted" and specifies: "dogs are allowed, kept on a leash". The beach is shown as open every day all year 2026, and access is free. The Côte d'Azur France and Gulf of Saint-Tropez websites, which reuse the tourist office page, say the same thing. No summer ban period is mentioned there, unlike neighbouring Grimaud, Cavalaire or La Croix-Valmer, where dogs are limited to certain hours in summer.</p>
      <p>Visit Var lists Le Treizain beach and La Moune beach among the Var's dog-friendly beaches: dogs kept on a leash allowed, waste to be picked up. The tourist office FAQ also reminds visitors that both beaches are open to dogs and that waste bags are available in the village.</p>
      <p>Pick up waste and follow the signs on site. The municipal beach police order is not published on the pages read: if in doubt, call the tourist office before you come in high season.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: arrive late in the afternoon to enjoy the view of the gulf with your dog, when the light on Saint-Tropez is at its best.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p><a href="/en/fiche-plage-moune-gassin">La Moune beach</a>, right next door, and the village of Gassin inland easily round off the outing.</p>
    </div>

